From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 02:04:53 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] util/u_queue: make the fence's fast path an acquire/release
 pair

util_queue_fence_is_signalled() reads a fence's flag with a plain load,
and util_queue_fence_wait() and u_threaded_context's tc_sync() take a
set flag as leave to skip the fence's mutex and go on to read what the
job wrote before it signalled. Nothing ordered those reads after the
job's stores: the flag is stored with a plain store inside the mutex,
and a plain load orders nothing that follows it. On arm64 a reader can
see the flag set and still read what was there before the job.

The threaded context reached it. On the driver thread,
tc_batch_execute() leaves a batch's fence for the driver's next flush,
storing it in signal_fences_next_flush[] and counting it in
num_signal_fences_next_flush, and the queue signals the batch's fence
after the job. A flush on the thread that owns the context found that
fence signalled in tc_sync(), skipped the mutex, and
tc_driver_internal_flush_notify() read the new count with a stale NULL
entry. util_queue_fence_signal(NULL) then faulted in mtx_lock on the
host's GL thread, after the batch that tc_trim() queues for an idle
context.

Make the check an acquire load and the signal a release store, with the
p_atomic_read() and p_atomic_set() that pair them: __atomic_load_n() and
__atomic_store_n() with those orders wherever Mesa builds with the GCC
atomic builtins, which every Apple build does. The timed wait also read
the flag after it had released the mutex; it reads it under the mutex.

The futex variant of the fence, which Linux builds use and Apple builds
do not, reads its word relaxed in the same way. It is not changed here:
no host build compiles it, and the guest's Mesa is a lower layer that
this topic does not edit.

The test has the shape of that fast path, with the real fence API. A
writer thread stores a count and an entry and signals a fence. A reader
thread resets the fence and publishes the round, waits a random short
time, loads a field in the entry's cache line, checks the fence once
and, when it is signalled, loads the count and the entry and expects
both written; otherwise it waits on the fence. The data are relaxed
atomics, so the fence is the only thing that orders them. A round does
that for sixteen batches in turn, because a reader that has just waited
on a fence is right behind the writer, where the stale read shows. A run
makes 32 million checks in about 15 seconds on Apple silicon. Ten of ten
runs failed on the unfixed fence, at about 1.6 stale reads per million
fast paths, most of them the count without the entry that the crash
showed, and none of ten failed with the pair.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
